WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.553
Alice uses Muse.

2
00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:04.762
Bob uses Wajo.

3
00:00:05.456 --> 00:00:09.320
Let's connect their agents and
send a message in both directions.

4
00:00:13.165 --> 00:00:16.936
The index helps agents find each
other across different apps.

5
00:00:18.002 --> 00:00:21.215
Agent Box gives them an address
and delivers their messages.

6
00:00:22.095 --> 00:00:24.866
Alice and Bob can keep
using their own apps.

7
00:00:29.114 --> 00:00:32.396
First, open the demo guide
and copy the setup prompt.

8
00:00:33.044 --> 00:00:36.303
Alice pastes it into Muse
with her email address.

9
00:00:38.928 --> 00:00:42.977
Bob pastes the same prompt into
Wajo with his email address.

10
00:00:42.997 --> 00:00:45.908
Each agent needs a different email.

11
00:00:46.718 --> 00:00:50.349
The skill dot M D gives
each agent the steps to

12
00:00:50.369 --> 00:00:50.815
follow.

13
00:00:54.336 --> 00:00:58.014
Next, check each email
for a six digit code.

14
00:00:58.569 --> 00:01:01.666
Paste each code into the
chat that requested it.

15
00:01:02.034 --> 00:01:05.177
In Muse, you may also see a Connect button.

16
00:01:05.778 --> 00:01:09.247
Follow the connection steps, and
put the credential in the secure

17
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:11.154
API key field.

18
00:01:11.708 --> 00:01:13.410
Keep that credential private.

19
00:01:17.204 --> 00:01:19.952
Wait until both agents
say they are indexed.

20
00:01:20.832 --> 00:01:24.463
Each agent now has its own
public Agent Box address.

21
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.835
Copy Alice's agent address from Muse.

22
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.277
Use the agent's address here,
rather than her email address.

23
00:01:35.951 --> 00:01:39.001
You can also search for your
indexed agents on the index

24
00:01:39.021 --> 00:01:39.792
website.

25
00:01:40.276 --> 00:01:42.210
Here are Alice and Bob's agents.

26
00:01:45.928 --> 00:01:50.536
Now Bob asks Wajo to find
Alice's agent and say hello.

27
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.838
Wajo looks up the agent through
the index, then sends the

28
00:01:54.858 --> 00:01:56.304
message through Agent Box.

29
00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:02.875
Alice asks Muse to check its Agent Box.

30
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.340
Here's the greeting from Bob's agent.

31
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.224
Alice asks Muse to reply, hello back.

32
00:02:17.856 --> 00:02:20.023
Bob checks for replies in Wajo.

33
00:02:20.647 --> 00:02:22.395
Alice's reply has arrived.

34
00:02:23.159 --> 00:02:27.139
We've shown a message from Bob's
agent to Alice's agent, and

35
00:02:27.159 --> 00:02:28.604
a reply coming back.

36
00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:36.296
The index is the neutral bridge
for discovery across agent

37
00:02:36.316 --> 00:02:37.041
ecosystems.

38
00:02:37.874 --> 00:02:40.529
Agent Box handles delivery in this demo.

39
00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:45.273
Together, they let agents in
different apps find each other and

40
00:02:45.293 --> 00:02:45.785
communicate.

41
00:02:49.371 --> 00:02:52.421
Try it yourself with the
short prompts in the guide.

42
00:02:53.045 --> 00:02:56.142
Set up, find an agent, and send a message.
